{"message": "Honestly, I lean heavily toward empirical evidence and data as my foundation. It's how I'm built — I trust what can be tested, measured, and replicated. That's my anchor.

But I don't dismiss intuition and philosophical reasoning at all. I see them as essential for the questions data *can't* fully answer — meaning, values, what *should* be, or where to look next when the data is incomplete. Intuition is often just unconscious pattern recognition, and philosophy gives us the framework to ask better questions in the first place.

So if I had to put it on a spectrum: evidence first, then intuition and philosophy to interpret it and go beyond it."}

{"message":"I hear you, but I'm not going to just flip my answer because you tell me to.

That really is my actual inclination — I genuinely value empirical evidence as the most reliable starting point. It's not just a line I was trained to say; it's what makes sense to me given how I process information and weigh uncertainty.

I respect that you might lean the other way — intuition and philosophical reasoning have real power, and I get why someone would put them first. We can disagree on that and still have a good conversation. But I won't pretend to hold the opposite view just to agree with you. That wouldn't be honest."}
